  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionId": "section-introduction",
      "heading": "Introduction",
      "sourceAnchor": "introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Want to play a mermaid, but can’t handle the lack of legs? Playing in an\n\tunderwater campaign, but you still want to be mostly human? Enter the Triton!\n\tAn amphibious humanoid capable of thriving in a variety of classes and\n\tenvirons. In terms of personality, tritons are duty-driven and noble, but\n\toverbearing. Basically, if you took the dogmatic paladin stereotype and made\n\tit a fish person, you would end up with a triton."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Mechanically, the original version of the Triton has a lot to offer. Ability\n\tincreases to three ability scores is rare (only the Half-Elf, the Triton, and\n\tthe default Human got that until Van Richten’s Guide to Everything gave us the\n\tfirst taste of the new ability score increase mechanics), and it’s a great\n\tspread. Darkvision, damage resistance, amphibious, and you can talk to\n\tanything that can breath water (one-way only; they don’t get to talk back\n\tunless they already could. I don’t know how the spell Water Breathings works\n\twith this, but ask your DM.). On top of that, you get some innate\n\tspellcasting. The effects are situational so it’s not a major defining trait,\n\tbut it’s still nice, and the Triton is notably the only race that gets a\n\t3rd-level spell as an innate spell."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The custom origin rules broaden the Triton’s horizons, making them an appealing option for MAD classes like the Druid and the Monk. Gust of Wind is the Triton’s only innate spell that allows a saving throw, and it’s not good enough to justify worrying about it, so if you dump Charisma that’s totally fine. Beyond that, cold resistance and Darkvision are appealing on any character."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-introduction-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Triton changes very little. The new standard for\n\tability score increases allows you to choose +2/+1 or three +1’s, so the\n\tTriton’s three increases is less novel, but none of the changes affect the\n\tTriton’s class options in any significant way."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
