    {
      "sectionId": "section-triton-versions",
      "heading": "Triton Versions",
      "sourceAnchor": "triton-versions",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-triton-versions-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Triton effectively has three versions. The original version of the Triton\n\twas published in Volo’s Guide to Monsters."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-triton-versions-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The introduction of the custom origin rules in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything\n\tgave us the second version of the Triton. Allowing players to rearrange their\n\tability score increases broadened their horizons beyond paladins."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-triton-versions-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Most recently, the Triton was updated and republished in Monsters of the\n\tMultiverse. The Triton’s innate spellcasting replaces Wall of Water with Water\n\tWalk and now allows you to pick your spellcasting ability and re-cast the\n\tspells using spell slots (the new standard), and the Triton now uses the new\n\tstandard for ability score increases. I don’t think there’s any exciting\n\treason for the change other than that WotC didn’t want to reprint Wall of\n\tWater. I think Wall of Water wasn’t a great spell, but it was still generally\n\tuseful enough as an innate spell, while Water Walk is extremely situational.\n"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-triton-versions-4",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Emissary of the Sea was updated to limit the creature type to beast,\n\telementals, and monstrosities. Between the innate spellcasting and the\n\tcreature type limitation, the changes aren’t huge, but I still consider them a\n\tsmall nerf."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-triton-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "heading": "Triton in the 2024 DnD Rules",
      "sourceAnchor": "triton-in-the-2024-dnd-rules",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-triton-in-the-2024-dnd-rules-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Triton were most recently updated in Monsters of the Multiverse, and have not yet been updated for the 2024 DnD rules. To play a Triton in the 2024 DnD rules, ignore the Ability Score Increases described in Monsters of the Multiverse. Instead, use the Ability Score Increases provided by your Background in the 2024 rules."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Triton Classes (Customizable Origins and MMoM)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-custom-origin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-custom-origin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re using the option updated versions of the race, including the “"
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Customizing Your Origin",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/tcoe/character-options#CustomizingYourOrigin"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything and/or the updated version published in "
            },
            {
              "type": "link",
              "text": "Monsters of the Multiverse",
              "href": "https://www.dndbeyond.com/sources/motm/fantastical-races"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ". Because the race changed so little between those two versions, I have decided to combine these two sections. If you’re not using those rules, scroll down to the “Classic Rules” section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Cold resistance, Darkvision, and some innate spellcasting are a good complement to the Artificer’s capabilities. The innate spellcasting isn’t amazing for the Artificer, but it helps."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Barbarians will struggle to make use of the innate spellcasting, but it’s mostly utility options anyway, and Darkvision and cold resistance are still decent."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Three ability score increases makes it easy to increase Dex/Con/Cha, but if you’re using the updated version of the Triton it’s also great to stick to Con/Cha. The innate spellcasting will complement the Bard’s limited number of spells known, but the spells aren’t ones that most characters will cast multiple times per day."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Since clerics generally don’t use weapons and therefore don’t need a high Str/Dex score, the updated version of the Triton has an advantage here. Cold resistance and Darkvision are both great, and the innate spellcasting is passable."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Cold resistance, Darkvision, and innate spellcasting are all great, but Fog Cloud, Gust of Wind, and Water Walk are all druid spells. Even Emissary of the Sea isn’t especially novel since you can often solve those same problems magically. The Triton adds very little that the Druid can’t already provide."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Fog Cloud is exciting if you take Fighting Style (Blind Fighting). It works very similarly to magical darkness, except that devils and warlocks still can’t see through it, placing you at a massive tactical advantage. If you’re using the Monsters of the Multiverse version of the Triton, an eldritch knight can re-cast it using spell slots, allowing you bring this tactic into play repeatedly at low cost. Of course, it only works at very short range and your allies won’t be able to see either, so use with caution."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-fighter-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Other varieties of fighter may struggle to make the Triton’s traits useful beyond cold resistance and Darkvision."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The custom origin version of the Triton is one of very few races with three +1 increases prior to the publication of the lineages in Van Richten’s Guide to Ravenloft and the updated races in Monsters of the Multiverse, so the Triton has some niche appeal if you want 16 in each of Dex/Con/Wis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Triton is less novel since everyone can get three +1 increases. With that level playing field, the Triton’s primary distinguishing traits is their innate spellcasting, which isn’t particularly useful for the Monk."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-monk-3",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In either case, cold resistance is nice, and Darkvision is excellent if you want to sneak around."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "With the flexibility offered by the custom origin rules or the updated version of the Triton, they’re no longer pigeon-holed as amphibious paladins. They do still make decent paladins, but there’s no unique or exciting rules interactions here. Cold resistance and Darkvision are always great, Emissary of the Sea offers an additional way to solve problems using your ample social skills, and innate spellcasting offers some additional utility options."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-paladin-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Triton can re-cast their innate spells using spell slots, but the spells aren’t so broadly useful that doing so is not a significant improvement."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Cold resistance and Darkvision are always great, Emissary of the Sea offers allows you to nonviolently handle some creatures that might otherwise require spells like Animal Friendship, and innate spellcasting offers some additional utility options."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-ranger-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Triton can re-cast their innate spells using spell slots, but the spells aren’t so broadly useful that doing so is not a significant improvement."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Cold resistance and Darkvision are great. The innate spellcasting is only situationally useful, but Fog Cloud does make a great escape mechanism if you’re off scouting and get into trouble."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The innate spellcasting offers some additional options to complement the Sorcerer’s limited number of spells known, but they’re not broadly useful enough for that to make a huge difference, so you’re mostly riding on cold resistance and Darkvision."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-sorcerer-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Triton can re-cast their innate spells using spell slots, but the spells aren’t so broadly useful that doing so is not a significant improvement."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Cold resistance and Darkvision are still great, but Darkvision won’t let you see through your own Fog Cloud and neither will Devil’s Sight."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-warlock-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Triton can re-cast their innate spells using spell slots, but the spells aren’t so broadly useful that doing so is not a significant improvement."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-custom-origin",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Cold resistance and Darkvision are both nice, but easily replaced with spells once you’re beyond low levels. The innate spellcasting is all spells on the Wizard’s spell list (with the exception of Wall of Water, but that’s no great loss). The Triton doesn’t bring anything to the wizard that you couldn’t already do."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-wizard-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The updated version of the Triton can re-cast their innate spells using spell slots, but the spells aren’t so broadly useful that doing so is not a significant improvement."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Triton Classes (Classic Rules)",
      "sourceAnchor": "classes-classic",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classes-classic-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "This section assumes that you’re "
            },
            {
              "type": "emphasis",
              "text": "not"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " using the option “Customizing Your Origin” rules presented in Tasha’s Cauldron of Everything or the updated version of the race published in Mordenkainen’s Monsters of the Multiverse. If you are using those rules, scroll up to the previous section."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-artificer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Artificer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-artificer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-artificer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-barbarian",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Barbarian",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-barbarian",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-barbarian-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength and Constitution are great, but the Barbarian has little use for\n\tCharisma and the innate spellcasting can’t be used while you’re raging."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-bard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Bard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-bard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-bard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Strength-based builds are extremely difficult for the Bard, but the\n\tConstitution and Charisma increases are great. If you can make it to level 3\n\tCollege of Valor will get you medium armor, so you can get by on just 14\n\tDexterity, but you may have better lucky simply ignoring your Strength\n\tincrease."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-cleric",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Cleric",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-cleric",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-cleric-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bad ability spread."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-druid",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Druid",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-druid",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-druid-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Bad ability spread."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-fighter",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Fighter",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-fighter",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-fighter-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Triton makes a fine fighter. Strength and Constitution are great, and the\n\tinnate spellcasting offer some useful options, but the Paladin is a better fit\n\tbecause it makes better use of the Triton’s ability score increases. The\n\tPurple Knight can still make good use of the Charisma increase, of course, but\n\tsince so few other fighter subclasses get Face skills or Charisma-based\n\tabilities it can be very difficult to bring Charisma into play."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-monk",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Monk",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-monk",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-monk-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Three ability increases, but none are to Dexterity or Wisdom."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-paladin",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Paladin",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-paladin",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-paladin-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Triton’s ability scores perfectly match what the Paladin needs. if you\n\tuse the “point buy” method for your ability scores, you could start with 16 in\n\tStrength, Constitution, and Charisma. That’s pretty great, but you’ll have 8s\n\teverywhere else which hurts."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-ranger",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Ranger",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-ranger",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-ranger-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The Triton’s ability scores don’t work great for the Ranger. Strength-based\n\tbuilds are possible, but they’re hard."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-rogue",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Rogue",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-rogue",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-rogue-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Dexterity increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-sorcerer",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Sorcerer",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-sorcerer",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-sorcerer-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Constitution and Charisma are a great start, and the combination of\n\tAmphibious and innate spellcasting will offer you some additional tools\n\twithout requiring you to learn those spells."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-warlock",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Warlock",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-warlock",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-warlock-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Constitution and Charisma are a great start, and for a Hexblade you could\n\tbuild around Strength instead of Dexterity if you’re brave enough to try it in\n\tmedium armor. Innate spellcasting is always welcome on warlocks due to their\n\tlimited spell slots."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "sectionId": "section-classic-wizard",
      "parentSectionId": "section-classes-classic",
      "heading": "Wizard",
      "sourceAnchor": "classic-wizard",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "blockId": "block-section-classic-wizard-1",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "No Intelligence increase."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
